   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the estates, powers and trusts law, in relation to payment of interest on delayed legacies to genetic children of the decedent",
    "basePrintNo": "S5160",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S5160-2015",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §11-A-2.1, EPT L",
    "lawSection": "Estates, Powers and Trusts Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S5160\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the estates, powers and trusts law, in relation to\npayment of interest on delayed legacies to genetic children of the\ndecedent\n\nPURPOSE OF BILL:\n\nThis bill establishes the commencement date for payment of a legacy\nwith interest to a genetic posthumous child of a decedent entitled to\ninherit under Section 4-1.3 of the Estates Powers and Trusts Law.\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS OF BILL:\n\nThis legislation amends paragraph 3 of Section 11-A-2.1 of the Estates\nPowers and Trusts Law (EPTL), as amended by Chapter 404 of the laws of\n2014 as follows:\n\nIf letters are not required interest upon a pecuniary gift left to a\ngenetic posthumous child who is entitled to inherit will commence upon\nthe later of seven months from the date of death (or date upon which\nthe beneficiary has a right to receive such payment) or the genetic\nchild's date of birth.\n\nIf letters are required, whether they are final, preliminary or\ntemporary, the right to interest on a pecuniary gift left to a genetic\nposthumous child who is entitled to inherit will commence upon the\nlater of seven months after the issuance of letters or the genetic\nchild's date of birth.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nWhen Chapter 404 of the Laws of 2014 (A.1185/5.4952) was drafted,\nthere were no provisions in law to allow a genetic posthumous child to\ninherit. Just prior to Chapter 404 becoming law, Chapter 439 of the\nLaws of 2014 (A.7461-A/S.04779-B) was signed by the Governor,\nestablishing a way for posthumous genetic children to inherit pursuant\nto the requirements of Section 4-1.3 of the EPTL. Therefore, it is\nnecessary to harmonize the provisions of Chapter 404 with those of\nChapter 439.\n\nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill, 2015.\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nNone.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\n\nThis act shall take effect immediately and shall be deemed to have\nbeen in full force and effect on and after December 20, 2014 and shall\napply to estates of the decedents who shall have died on or after such\ndate.\n\n\n",
